itional gambling  systems for inspiration when designing  loot boxes that's why they often look  like slot machines cards dice roulette  wheels and so on the similarities aren't  surface level either loot boxes use  variable ratio schedules of  reinforcement which is a fancy way of  saying that because rewards are dished  out at random you're incentivized to  keep opening boxes in the hope that the  next box will give you what you want  research has shown this variable ratio  schedule is the best way to keep us  hooked and if that sounds a lot like a  slot machine well that's because it's  literally exactly how a slot machine  works weirdly there's not loads of stuff  in here about developers being inspired  by chocolate eggs  it also shows that for players who  engage with loot boxes their motivations  track very closely with problem gambling  behaviors things like the excitement of  opening boxes peer pressure and the  feeling of being motivated by Temptation  and that the majority of loot box  revenue is generated from a small number  of individuals who spend a lot of money  and score highly on problem gambling  measures also known as Wales essentially  this report suggests that many loot box  systems are aesthetically and  mechanically inspired by gambling they  motivate engagement in the same way as  gambling and they carry the same  potential for harm as gambling does now  when I first read this report I assumed  this was going to be the Smoking Gun for  loot boxes and chart a clear path  towards regulation but as it turns out  it's not quite that simple this is Pete  etchels he's a professor of psychology  and science communication at bath spa  University and he's also written lost in  a good game a book about the  psychological effects good and bad of  video games we've seen a very distant  line that the more you spend on loot  boxes the more likely you are to score  highly on measures of problem gambling  whether that translates into harm or not  is a different question we don't know  whether it's that we spend more on loot  boxes that drives you to become more  problematic in terms of gambling habit  if that's the case then loot boxes are  basically a Gateway mechanism for  problem gambling or it could be that  people who have a higher propensity  towards problem gambling are more drawn  to games with loot boxes in them and  these sorts of mechanisms are targeting  vulnerable populations so either way  it's not good but there's a third option  as well which is that there's a third  variable that we haven't thought about  yet that is driving both either in  somebody's life or in the game itself or  combination of the two that makes people  spend more money on loot boxes and also  makes them more problematic gamblers and  if that's the case then loot boxes  aren't necessarily the problem in that  scenario if you've ever wondered why  loot boxes and other random reward  systems aren't better regulated despite  their obvious similarities to gambling  this lack of causation is why until  research can prove that loot boxes  directly cause problem gambling  behaviors it's essentially down to the  player to understand any potential risk  and decide for themselves whether to  play the game and engage with these  systems or not  [Music]  everybody is equipped to make that  decision many games that feature loot  boxes are rated as age-appropriate for  children and according to the Gambler  wear report 25 to 50 percent of children  in adolescents have opened a loot box  which would suggest that whatever  measures are in place to stop children  from interacting with loot boxes just  aren't working this is Alexander Chris  he's a clinical psychologist and author  of The Gaming mind and he's an advocate  for the psychological benefits of  playing games he is not a fan of loot  boxes I don't like I don't like them and  so I have avoided buying them or  participating in that myself and from a  psychological standpoint I see loot  boxes as kind of like violating some of  the basic terms of play my concern more  lies like on the game developer side  right of what is their responsibility in  sort of providing an experience that is  intended to be engaging and entertaining  and meaningful in some way versus you  know a cash grab that maybe kind of  plays on our worst Natures in a sense  many Publishers will point to the fact  that you can play these modes without  spending any money but they often  require a huge time commitment instead  sure when Star Wars Battlefront 2 came  out you could technically unlock Darth  Vader for free but it would be in  exchange for around 40 hours of play  time to generate enough credits to  unlock one character  wouldn't it be so much easier to just  buy a loot box and get the credits that  way instead it's almost like that's what  their plan was the whole time what is  our responsibility as Citizens as  parents as people who are making games  to keep in mind the most vulnerable  people who are going to be using the  game I think that in some ways part of  my concerns about all this is almost  defensive because I've spent part of my  professional career sort of trying to  defend games in a certain way as this  thing that's not as dangerous as people  think they don't reach out and grab you  and turn you into a murderer right but  if a young person yeah goes into a game  and they walk away having spent hundreds  of dollars of their parents money  without maybe understanding even what  that means then it is a social problem  right it's not just a game problem it is  like bad for games reputation if we know  that some vulnerable people are going to  come into contact with a system that can  prey on vulnerable people what is our  responsibility to think about how those  systems work how they're regulated and  so on it's worth returning to to that  statistic that between 25 and 50 percent  of children have opened the loot box  Children and adolescents are especially  vulnerable to feelings of peer pressure  the fear of missing out and wanting a  quick fix solution particularly when the  potential rewards are only available for  a limited time however research has  shown that this also applies to other  vulnerable groups such as those with  ADHD impulse control disorders and of  course those who already suffer from  problematic gambling behaviors where  video games were once considered a kind  of safe space these mechanics have the  potential to undercut that safety for  the very people who are most at risk we  look at the effects that video games can  have on us and by and large positive or  negative video games haven't got much of  an impact until you start changing the  games themselves how are those sorts of  mechanisms are implemented can change  our our opinion or our barometer or  video game effects really not being much  to worry about to potentially being  quite problematic we thought games were  fine and then you you literally stuck a  casino in a game and now we know that  games maybe are not fine because there's  a casino in them  so what's stopping loot boxes being  regulated unsurprisingly it's a  complicated issue often games will  reference random reward mechanics in  their ESRB or Peggy rating  specifications but these do not impact  the actual age rating of the game Peggy  also offers Parental Guidance which  states parents carers or other  responsible adults should check to see  what is being offered before making any  purchase on behalf of a child which  simply shifts the blame for any harm  onto the shoulders appearance besides  the warning on the packaging is  absolutely minuscule there is a reason  why you know you're not allowed into a  casino if you're under 18. if a 12 year  old has their parents credit card that  doesn't mean that they could get into a  casino and like it's the parents fault  right like if the casino lets a child in  whether they have a way to pay or not  it's on them right that's that's the  regulation That's the Law if the people  running the game could be blamed because  a 10 year old got a hold of their  parents credit card then they would  instantly create new safeguards to make  sure that the people who were engaging  in this stuff were at least  hypothetically people who had the  capacity to consent to taking these  risks or sort of participating in this  way the fact is that regulatory bodies  are simply not agile enough to keep up  with the industry some countries have  passed legislation but simple changes  such as giving away loot box gifts with  every purchase of in-game currency allow  total evasion of the legislation with  the result being little to no effect on  the financial performance of loot boxes  or the demographic interacting with them  in 2017 Belgium ruled that loot boxes  constituted unlicensed gambling and  banned them entirely but the ban has  proven almost impossible to enforce as  most loot boxes do not allow you to cash  out or exchange the rewards for anything  that's traditionally deemed of value in  many countries they cannot legally be  considered gambling at all any more than  buying a pack of Pokemon cards or I  suppose a chocolate egg now this might  will sound pretty bleak but there are  reason to be optimistic the fact that so  many governments Charities and  psychologists around the world are  researching loot boxes and their effects  is a good thing regardless of whether or  not it directly leads to change many  loot box studies are pre-registered  meaning they say in advance how they're  going to collect and analyze their data  and many of these studies make their  data openly available meaning there's an  increasingly Rich ever expanding set of  data to use in research in the future  the more we understand about loot boxes  and their effects the more likely we are  to end up with a future where the  harmful elements of loot boxes are  minimized without compromising the  opportunity for free-to-play developers  to fund their game from well-informed  consenting adults and we're closer to  that future now than ever before we're  not talking anymore about our games good  or bad for us because it's a stupid  question to ask and nobody cares are  there particular aspects of games or are  there particular mechanisms that are  implemented in games that if you take  them out or add them in do you see a net  Improvement in well-being or net  reduction that understanding will help  to inform a less psychologically  problematic way of monetizing games and  I think that's a really interesting area  of research right and as we become more  Savvy about doing research in this area  and we start to kind of engage a little  bit more with industry we'll be able to  do more useful research like that  thanks so much for watching this week's  episode of Mind Games next week we're  covering the game that fights dementia  so make sure you're subscribed and check  your feeds for that next week for more  information on the guests and the topics  covered in this video check the  description down below and I'll see you  next time

